5:["$","$L10",null,{"study":{"id":"shreya","title":"A post, a prosecution, and free expression","category":"Landmark judgment","year":"2015","subtitle":"Shreya Singhal v. Union of India","fictional":false,"difficulty":"Law & rights","summary":"The Supreme Court examined section 66A and the intermediary framework, distinguishing discussion, advocacy, and incitement.","story":["Public interest litigation challenged arrests and prosecutions connected with online expression under section 66A of the IT Act. The petitions raised the provision’s breadth and the chilling effect of vague criminal restrictions on speech.","The Court distinguished discussion, advocacy, and incitement in its free-speech analysis. It held section 66A unconstitutional, finding that the provision did not fit the constitutionally permissible restriction framework for the relevant public-order analysis.","The decision also addressed intermediary liability and interpreted actual knowledge in the context of court orders or appropriate government notification. Later rules must be read with the statutory framework and the judgment, not as a license to disregard it."],"timeline":[{"date":"2012","event":"Arrests and controversy bring section 66A under public scrutiny."},{"date":"2015","event":"Supreme Court decides the constitutional challenges."},{"date":"Later years","event":"Official guidance addresses discontinuation of prosecutions under the invalidated provision."}],"issue":"Whether section 66A validly restricted expression and how intermediary safe harbour should be understood.","holding":"Section 66A was struck down. The decision is a central authority on expression and intermediary liability, not a declaration that all online conduct is immune from prosecution.","lessons":["Read the actual holding, not a social-media summary.","Vagueness and overbreadth matter in penal restrictions.","Other valid offences may still apply to unlawful conduct.","Check current rules and later judgments for operational duties.","Do not use section 66A as a current charge."],"questions":["Why is a printed bare Act not always a complete statement of current law?","How does the judgment distinguish expression from unlawful conduct?","What records would you keep of a platform’s legal basis for removal?"],"sources":["shreya","itact","it2026"],"topics":["1.2","1.3","1.11"]}}]
